  </Placemark>
  <Placemark>
    <name>Sivells Bend United Methodist Church</name>
    <styleUrl>#normal</styleUrl>
    <description><![CDATA[CR 437 at FM1201<br/><br/><b>Address:</b> Sivells Bend<br/><br/><b>County:</b> Cooke<br/><br/><b>Marker Text:</b> This congregation formed in 1868 as Sivells Bend Methodist Episcopal Church, South. Brothers William W. and Addison Y. Gunter conveyed two acres to church trustees E. L. Allen, WM. Midkiff and George Dillard in 1880. A large two-story church, school and masonic lodge burned in 1893. W. W. Gunter deeded the current site in 1910. The present frame church has had continuous services since its 1912 construction. During WWII, families taught Sunday school even without a supplied pastor. Later, Sivells Bend shared pastors with other Methodist churches, but has had its own pastors since 1950. Community involvement and regular worship shows a continuing focus on the true spirit of God.]]></description>
    <Point><coordinates>-97.230401,33.843868,0</coordinates></Point>
  </Placemark>
  <Placemark>
    <name>Van Slyke Cemetery</name>
    <styleUrl>#normal</styleUrl>
    <description><![CDATA[N on FM 1199, W on CR 446, N on CR 453, 5 mi. NW of Lindsay<br/><br/><b>Address:</b> Lindsay<br/><br/><b>County:</b> Cooke<br/><br/><b>Marker Text:</b> Van Slyke Cemetery Established 1883 Historic Texas Cemetery - 2005]]></description>
    <Point><coordinates>-97.277133,33.695378,0</coordinates></Point>
  </Placemark>
  <Placemark>
    <name>Ater Cemetery</name>
    <styleUrl>#normal</styleUrl>
    <description><![CDATA[from Gatesville, go 1 mi. W on US 84, turn rt. on FM 2412; after approx. 10 mi. turn rt. on CR 174 &amp; go N on winding paved road 1.8 mi. to cemetery<br/><br/><b>Address:</b> Gatesville<br/><br/><b>County:</b> Coryell<br/><br/><b>Marker Text:</b> Originally founded as Sardis, the town of Ater took its name from store owner Joe Ater (1856-1928), who also served as its only postmaster from 1899 to 1907. A Mr. Dooley and Lemuel Murrell reportedly deeded land for church, school and cemetery purposes during the community's formative years. The 1868 gravestones of two young women, Elvista and Leovisa Haley, attest to the early use of this cemetery by area settlers. Veterans laid to rest here served in the War of 1812, the Civil War, World War I, World War II and Korea. An association oversees the care of this site that records the heritage of the once-active community of Ater. Historic Texas Cemetery-2002.]]></description>
    <Point><coordinates>-97.860367,31.521605,0</coordinates></Point>
  </Placemark>
  <Placemark>
    <name>Callicoatte, John Bailey</name>
    <styleUrl>#normal</styleUrl>
    <description><![CDATA[Bee House Cemetery,from Evant take Fm 183 SE about 7 mi to Spur 183, go west .1 mi to county &amp; follow west about 1 mi, Bee House.<br/><br/><b>Address:</b> Bee House<br/><br/><b>County:</b> Coryell]]></description>
    <Point><coordinates>-98.080330,31.399035,0</coordinates></Point>
