  <Placemark>
    <name>Officers Quarters No. 8</name>
    <styleUrl>#normal</styleUrl>
    <description><![CDATA[Fort Concho National Historic Landmark, S side of parade ground.<br/><br/><b>Address:</b> 630 S. Oakes St., San Angelo<br/><br/><b>County:</b> Tom Green]]></description>
    <Point><coordinates>-100.429118801789,31.4533315466187,0</coordinates></Point>
  </Placemark>
  <Placemark>
    <name>Adams-Ziller House</name>
    <styleUrl>#normal</styleUrl>
    <description><![CDATA[SW corner Guadalupe and W. 14th streets. RTHL medallion displayed, marker in storage.<br/><br/><b>Address:</b> 1306 Guadalupe, Austin<br/><br/><b>County:</b> Travis<br/><br/><b>Marker Text:</b> Augusta Adams (ca. 1817-1907), a widow and native of Mecklenberg, Germany, immigrated to Texas and purchased land at this site in 1857. This modest structure was constructed circa 1868. Augusta later married Herman Hertel. Her two daughters, Mary and Theresa Adams, married brothers August and William Ziller, sons of one of Austin’s earliest settlers, Michael Ziller. The home remained in the Ziller family until 1955, and was saved from demolition during the 1990s. The one-story residence features a center-passage plan with chimneys on the gable ends, and buff brick laid in a common bond pattern.]]></description>
    <Point><coordinates>-97.74359,30.276757,0</coordinates></Point>
  </Placemark>
  <Placemark>
    <name>Austin Lodge No. 12, A.F. &amp; A.M.</name>
    <styleUrl>#normal</styleUrl>
    <description><![CDATA[Scottish Rite Temple, on exhibit in library. Marker reported in storage Nov. 2016.<br/><br/><b>Address:</b> 207 W. 18th St., Austin<br/><br/><b>County:</b> Travis<br/><br/><b>Marker Text:</b> Master masons gathered at the home of Edwin Waller, surveyor of the townsite of Austin, on Oct. 11, 1839, one week before President Mirabeau B. Lamar arrived to set up government in the new Republic of Texas capital. Grand Master branch archer authorize the organization of Austin Lodge No. 12 on Nov. 7, 1839. Alexander Russell was the first Worshipful Master. Many prominent Texans have been members of this body. Edward Burleson, who served as vice president of the Republic of Texas, was a Lodge officer. His burial with Masonic honors in 1851 was the 1st interment in the state cemetery. 2 Texas governors, Edward Clark, and Harden R. Runnels, were members of the Lodge. In the early years, meetings were held at various sites. A fire in 1866 destroyed the lodges, quarters, and burned some of its records. In 1883 members moved into the new Masonic Temple at 7th and Colorado. The Scottish Rite Temple at 18th and Lavaca served as the meeting place from 1923 to 1968, when the Lodge moved to a new facility on Bull Creek Rd. During its history, Austin Lodge No. 12 has responded to human need in times of natural disaster. Members have participated in the dedications of a number of Austin's public buildings and church structures. (1979)]]></description>
    <Point><coordinates>-97.740667,30.279948,0</coordinates></Point>
  </Placemark>
  <Placemark>
    <name>Baker Family Cemetery</name>
    <styleUrl>#normal</styleUrl>
    <description><![CDATA[Located 3.9 miles west of Cedar Park at 12308 Lime Creek Road.  From Cedar Park take Hwy 1431 west for 1.5 miles; turn left on Lime Creek Road and go 2.4 miles; the cemetery is behind a gate on the north side of Lake Creek Road.<br/><br/><b>Address:</b> Lago Vista<br/><br/><b>County:</b> Travis<br/><br/><b>Marker Text:</b> Baker Family Cemetery Established 1903 Historic Texas Cemetery-2001]]></description>
    <Point><coordinates>-97.868838,30.483222,0</coordinates></Point>
  </Placemark>
  <Placemark>
    <name>Banks-Wood Cemetery</name>
    <styleUrl>#normal</styleUrl>
    <description><![CDATA[From Webberville, take TX Farm Road 969 1 mile east to intersection of Blake Manor Road and TX Farm Road 969, turn left and travel Blake Manor Road 8/10 miles to 21329 Blake Manor Road. Gate is on left at curve in road on Harmony Ranch. The cemetery is located approximately 1,000 feet from Blake Manor Road down dirt road in Harmony Ranch, a property owned by the City of Austin. Gates will be lock due to this being an active ranch. Call Austin Energy at 512-322-6522 for access to the cemetery.<br/><br/><b>Address:</b> Blake Manor Road, Webberville<br/><br/><b>County:</b> Travis]]></description>
    <Point><coordinates>-97.496468900396,30.241847101491,0</coordinates></Point>
  </Placemark>
  <Placemark>
    <name>Beriah Graham House</name>
    <styleUrl>#normal</styleUrl>
    <description><![CDATA[<b>Address:</b> 2605 Salado, Austin<br/><br/><b>County:</b> Travis<br/><br/><b>Marker Text:</b> Recorded Texas Historic Landmark - 1962 (medallion only)]]></description>
